5BSD import status
==================

Upstream:  Protocol Buffers 29.6, https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf
Archive:   https://github.com/protocolbuffers/protobuf/releases/download/v29.6/protobuf-29.6.tar.gz
SHA256:    877bf9f880631aa31daf2c09896276985696728137fcd43cc534a28c5566d9ba
           (equal to the devel/protobuf port's distinfo)
License:   LICENSE (BSD-3-Clause, Google); third_party/utf8_range/LICENSE
           (MIT, Yibo Cai and Google).  Code that protoc generates is owned
           by the owner of the input file (LICENSE).

Trimmed to what base builds (tools/build/protobuf/trim.py): the C++
runtime (libprotobuf, libprotobuf-lite), libprotoc without the Java,
Kotlin, C#, Objective-C, PHP, Python, Ruby and Rust generators, upb with
its C, mini-table and reflection generators and their checked-in bootstrap
sources, utf8_range, the well-known and compiler .proto files and the
version scripts.  Not imported: tests, benchmarks, conformance, the other
language runtimes, hpb, the bundled third_party submodules and every
build system.

Local changes (tools/build/protobuf/patches):
  protobuf-default-proto-path.patch	protoc looks in
	<prefix>/include/private for the well-known types.
  protobuf-upb-defpool-oom.patch	upb checks the arena allocations
	of upb_DefPool_New() and of loading compiled-in definitions, which
	crashed on allocation failure.

Built by lib/protobuf (libprivateprotobuf, libprivateprotobuf-lite, the
internal libprotoc and libupb), usr.bin/protoc (installed as
/usr/libexec/protoc, off the default PATH, per lib/protobuf/protobuf.dirs.mk;
a 5BSD driver that registers the C++ generator only) and
libexec/protoc-gen-upb,
protoc-gen-upb_minitable and protoc-gen-upbdefs; lib/protobuf/protoc-gen.mk
generates code at build time with the copies buildworld bootstraps.
Installed licence: /usr/share/licenses/protobuf.
